{% trace-scenario title="Name the opening decision" prompt="Give the reader a concrete production situation with a meaningful choice." outcome="Explain what evidence should drive the decision and why." /%}

## At a glance

![Describe the article-specific decision map and every meaningful stage](/media/specific-production-problem-orientation.png)

State the reader, their job, the outcome, and the article boundary. Use {% highlight %}one consequential phrase{% /highlight %} to establish the core decision without highlighting an entire paragraph.

### The operating model

Name a small, topic-specific framework. Explain the sequence in one clear paragraph and show how it will organize the article.

| Stage | Evidence | Reader decision |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Context | What the system and user are trying to accomplish | Define success |
| Signal | What changed or failed | Choose what to inspect |
| Action | What the evidence supports | Change, hold, or escalate |

## Start with the user outcome

Teach the first major decision with enough depth to be useful. Replace every scaffold paragraph with original, source-grounded material.

### Separate symptoms from the decision

Carry one concrete example through the article. Explain the expected path, the first meaningful divergence, and the user-visible consequence.

> **Field note:** Use a short note to preserve an operating lesson the reader should remember during implementation.

## Build the evidence chain

Alternate explanation with proof. A long article should not become an uninterrupted chain of headings and paragraphs.

{% editorial-panel eyebrow="Operating principle" title="State the rule that changes the decision." body="Explain the evidence, owner, threshold, and response that make the rule operational." tone="yellow" /%}

### Show the implementation boundary

The code must match the current repository or be labeled illustrative. Explain inputs, expected output, failure behavior, and the production boundary around it.

```ts
// Illustrative: replace with a topic-specific, verified example.
type Decision = "proceed" | "hold" | "escalate";

export function chooseNextMove(signal: number, threshold: number): Decision {
  if (!Number.isFinite(signal)) return "escalate";
  return signal >= threshold ? "proceed" : "hold";
}
```

## Work through a realistic example

Use inputs and outputs that make the framework adaptable. Explain why a tempting shortcut fails and what a safer decision looks like.

| Observation | Weak interpretation | Better next move |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Topic-specific signal | A plausible but incomplete conclusion | The evidence-led action |
| Boundary condition | A shortcut that hides uncertainty | A bounded review or test |

## Diagnose the first divergence

Explain how traces, logs, evaluations, or user outcomes narrow the problem. Use {% highlight %}a precise diagnostic phrase{% /highlight %} where it helps the reader scan.

{% editorial-panel eyebrow="Diagnostic shortcut" title="Give the reader a memorable way to find the smallest useful fix." body="Describe the order in which evidence should be read and the symptom that should not distract them." tone="dark" /%}

## Compare the meaningful trade-offs

Cover quality, reliability, cost, latency, safety, and operator effort only where they apply. Do not hide a serious regression inside a composite score.

> **Decision rule:** State the condition that causes a release, hold, rollback, or human review.

## Protect the security boundary

Describe permissions, secrets, tenant boundaries, side effects, unsafe inputs, and approval requirements relevant to this topic. State what the example deliberately cannot authorize.

## Read the lower half visually

![Describe a unique lower-half teaching visual that explains the article’s hardest relationship](/media/specific-production-problem-decision.png)

Keep the second half as composed as the opening. Use another table, a worked scenario, and {% highlight %}at least two meaningful lower-half highlights{% /highlight %} across the completed article.

### Explain what changes the recommendation

Name the conditions under which the preferred approach stops being correct. Give the reader a way to recognize those conditions.

## Make the operating decision

{% editorial-panel eyebrow="Decision room" title="Summarize the evidence-led outcome without flattening the trade-offs." body="Show what ships, what remains blocked, who owns the next step, and what evidence closes the gap." tone="paper" /%}

Carry the worked example to a decision. Link naturally to at least two distinct, relevant published Uncoil articles inside teaching sentences—not in a recommendation dump.

## Leave the reader with the next move

- [ ] The success condition is explicit.
- [ ] The example and code are verified or honestly labeled.
- [ ] The failure, security, and rollback boundaries are observable.
- [ ] The owner, threshold, and next action are recorded.
- [ ] The rendered article has been inspected from the opening through the FAQ on desktop and mobile.

Finish with an actionable paragraph. Do not add an in-body “Recommended next reads” heading; shared related posts render after the article.

## Frequently asked questions

{% faq-item question="What is the primary decision this approach supports?" answer="Give a concise answer grounded in the article’s operating model." /%}
{% faq-item question="When should a team use a different approach?" answer="Name the most important boundary or exception." /%}
{% faq-item question="How much evidence is enough to act?" answer="Explain the sample, confidence, or verification rule without false precision." /%}
{% faq-item question="How often should this be reviewed in production?" answer="Tie the cadence to releases, drift, incidents, and user impact." /%}
